# Writing style

No em dashes (`—`) or en dashes (`–`) in this repo: README, comments, commits, module docs, chat about this project.

Use plain ASCII:
- Colon for labels (`Damage tracking: needsRedraw gates...`)
- Period, comma, or parentheses for asides
- Hyphen (`-`) only for compounds and ranges (`zero-allocation`)

Docs: short sentences, plain words, one idea per bullet. Minimal example before edge cases. Tighten; do not expand unless asked. Diagrams: `->` or "to".

# Comments

Write for a reader who has never seen this repository.

- Say what the code does and why, in the present tense. Never what it used to do, what it replaced, or which bug led to it: that goes in the commit message and the changelog.
- Every module has a header, and every exported name has a Haddock comment (`cabal haddock` lists what is missing).
- Give the facts the code cannot: units, coordinate space, when a result is `Nothing`, who owns a pointer, why a step has to come first.
- Define a project term where it first matters, or use plain words.
- Do not restate the name. Do not pad: no "This function", "simply", "robust", "in order to", "note that".
